# Project Spec — Arabic Legal Q&A System (Egypt + Saudi Arabia)

Complete build specification. Hand this to Claude Code (or any coding agent) at the
start of the project.

Every phase ends in a **phase gate** with concrete acceptance criteria. Stop at each
gate, show me results, and wait for confirmation before continuing.

---

## Context for the agent

I am building a personal research tool that lets me ask questions in Arabic or English
about Egyptian and Saudi law, and get answers grounded in the actual text of statutes,
with exact article citations.

I am not a lawyer. The tool exists to help me arrive at a lawyer's office already
knowing which law and which article is relevant — it is not a substitute for legal
advice, and it must never present itself as one.

This is a solo side project. Prefer boring, debuggable technology over frameworks.
I want to understand every line.

---

## Non-negotiable design rules

1. **Article-level chunking.** Statutes are already divided into numbered articles.
   Chunk on that boundary. Never split an article across chunks; never merge two
   articles into one chunk.

2. **Every answer cites law number + year + article number.** No citation, no claim.

3. **Refusal beats guessing.** If retrieval confidence is low, the correct output is
   "I could not find this in the corpus." Hallucinated article numbers are the primary
   failure mode of legal RAG and they look completely convincing.

4. **Jurisdiction is a hard pre-filter, not a ranking signal.** Egyptian and Saudi legal
   Arabic uses near-identical vocabulary for materially different rules. A query scoped
   to Egypt must never retrieve Saudi text.

5. **Temporal validity is tracked from day one.** Every article carries effective dates
   and a repealed flag. Saudi Arabia is mid-codification (Personal Status Law 2022,
   Civil Transactions Law 2023), so stale text is a live risk, not a theoretical one.

6. **Identical Arabic normalization on both indexed text and queries.** Mismatched
   normalization silently destroys recall and produces no error message.

7. **No LangChain, no LlamaIndex, no agent frameworks.** Write the retrieval loop
   directly — roughly 300 lines. Framework abstractions hide retrieval bugs.

8. **Raw files are immutable.** Save every downloaded file to disk untouched, keyed by
   source URL and fetch date. Re-parsing is free; re-crawling is not.

