## Data sources

**Saudi Arabia**
- `laws.boe.gov.sa` — Bureau of Experts at the Council of Ministers. Statutes in Arabic
  plus official English translations. Clean text, no OCR needed.
- `ncar.gov.sa` — National Center for Archives and Records
- Ministry of Justice monthly circulars on amendments

**Egypt — start with the `fr3on` datasets on Hugging Face**

These are purpose-built for exactly this task and may remove most of the acquisition
and OCR work. Inspect all of them before writing a single scraper.

- **`fr3on/eg-legal-rag`** — the primary candidate. Load it first and inspect its actual
  schema before assuming anything about its shape. Report back: field names, row count,
  whether articles are already split at article boundaries, and whether law
  number/year/article number are present as structured fields or only embedded in text.
  If it is already article-segmented with usable metadata, Phase 1's parser work shrinks
  dramatically — say so and we will re-plan.
- **`fr3on/eg-legal-instruction-following`** — 4,184 instruction-style examples over
  Egyptian criminal, civil, procedural, and personal status law. Apache 2.0. Best used
  as raw material for the eval gold set: mine it for realistic question phrasing, then
  hand-verify the expected articles rather than trusting its labels.
- Check the rest of the `fr3on` profile for other Egyptian legal releases and list what
  you find. Also on the profile: `egyptian-dialogue` and `egyptian-songs` — not legal,
  but `egyptian-dialogue` is worth noting if I later want the system to handle questions
  asked in colloquial Egyptian rather than Modern Standard Arabic.

**Validation gate on any of these before building on them:** pick one statute I know
well, compare 20 articles character-by-character against the official gazette or
Tashreaat text, and report a fidelity estimate. A corpus that is 95% right is still
wrong in ways that will surface as confidently miscited law.

- `dataflare/egypt-legal-corpus` — ~25M tokens of Egyptian legal text, MIT licensed,
  with hierarchical category metadata. Good fallback or cross-check if the `fr3on`
  coverage has gaps.
- `TawasulAI/egyptian-law-articles` — another article-level Egyptian set worth comparing
- `tashreaat.com` (LADIS) — laws, decrees, some court rulings. Use as the authority to
  check the datasets against, and to fill gaps.
- Al-Waqai al-Misriyya (Official Gazette) for authoritative text
- Court of Cassation Technical Bureau for rulings. Egypt has no systematic case
  reporter and coverage is incomplete — never assume the corpus is exhaustive.

